#
# A logic:CheckpointRestore whose folded identity DISAGREES with the checkpoint it
# restores. Every record here is individually impeccable: the checkpoint carries its
# fold and names its run, and the restore carries its own fold and resumes that same
# run. Nothing a check over one record could see is wrong.
#
# What it costs in the world: the checkpoint was taken under engine v1; the restoring
# process folds its seven current axes and gets a v2 value, because the rules moved
# under it between the pause and the resume. Resuming anyway produces a result the
# paused run would never have produced, and the journal records it as a continuation
# of the run that was reviewed. The fold is the single value that makes that drift
# visible BEFORE the first resumed step executes, which is why the comparison is a law
# and not a note.
#
# It is the mismatch case, not the absence case: the absence sibling
# (checkpoint-no-folded-identity.ttl) can only reach
# logic:CheckpointCarriesFoldedIdentityConstraint, because the comparison law needs
# both folds bound before its guard binds at all.
#
# MUST trip logic:CheckpointRestoreIdentityConstraint.

@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix ex:    <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/examples/logic/tests/> .

ex:resumeWeek13 a logic:CheckpointRestore ;
    rdfs:label "a resume whose engine folded to a different identity than the pause did"@en ;
    logic:restoresCheckpoint ex:ckptWeek13 ;
    logic:restoreOfEnactment ex:adrReviewWeek13 ;
    logic:restoreDescriptorHash "b3:2f7d90c4a1e58b36f0d2c47a91be5308df6a24c0b93e71fa58d0c3629e4b17a5" .

ex:ckptWeek13 a logic:EnactmentCheckpoint ;
    rdfs:label "the pause point, folded under the engine as it stood then"@en ;
    logic:checkpointOfEnactment ex:adrReviewWeek13 ;
    logic:checkpointDescriptorHash "b3:8c15a02e7fb349d6015c8a37e04b2f91d6708ca3e52b19f7460d8b23a1c095e4" .
